{
  "_doc": "#1484/#1537 THE file-type to viewer/editor mapping, as data. `viewers[].id` names the lib that owns the file (wysiwyg = libs:wysiwyg, json = libs:json-viewer, media = libs:media-viewer, pdf = libs:pdf, odf = libs:odf, hex = editor-core's own hex dump); `formats` maps a format the viewer understands to the extensions that carry it. `modes` (View, Edit, Served) lists the modes a viewer offers, `format_modes` overrides it per format: for Markdown and HTML View is the rendered WYSIWYG view, editable; Edit is the raw source file; Served (HTML only) shows the page from a loopback-only local server (libs:local-serve) so relative CSS, JS and images resolve; for an image Edit is rotate, crop and annotate; SVG is shown from the loopback server and edited as source. A viewer with the single mode View is view only (audio, video). `unsupported` is checked FIRST: the Microsoft Office formats are refused on purpose and answer `unsupported format`. #1537 A name no entry matches (no extension, or an unknown one) is never unsupported: `sniff` reads the first `sample_bytes` of the file, a `magic` entry claims known containers by their leading bytes (each `match` part is an offset and hex bytes, all must match), otherwise a UTF-8 / ASCII heuristic (no NUL, control bytes under `max_control_ratio`, printable ASCII at least `min_printable_ascii_ratio` when the bytes are not valid UTF-8) sends it to `sniff.text` and everything else to `sniff.binary` (the hex view). Adding a type, a magic number or a mode is a data edit.",
